-</style><style data-v54-caption-keep>.book44 .journey-table caption{break-after:avoid!important;page-break-after:avoid!important}.book44 .journey-table thead{break-before:avoid!important;page-break-before:avoid!important}</style></head><body data-sact-editorial-v44="0.84.44" class="book44"><main><section class="book44-chapter" id="book44-treatment"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="1"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 01</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Understanding your treatment</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">Why this treatment is offered, what its medicines do, and how the agreed plan fits your care.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>National Cancer Control Programme (NCCP) 00374</p></header><section class="pk-section" id="urgent"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Act on symptoms, not the calendar</div><h2>When to get help</h2><div class="pk-note urgent"><h3>Emergency: call 112 / 999</h3><p>Severe breathing difficulty, collapse, seizure or severe chest pain: call emergency services. Do not wait for an online answer.</p></div><div class="pk-note urgent"><h3>Contact your oncology service immediately</h3><p>Temperature 37.5°C or higher, 36°C or lower, shivering or suddenly feeling unwell — even with a normal temperature. Call oncology immediately for fever, shaking chills, suddenly feeling unwell, unusual bleeding, new breathlessness, very little urine, severe rash or vomiting that prevents drinking. Follow your hospital’s temperature and contact instructions. Severe breathing difficulty, chest pain, collapse or seizures: call 112/999. Marked muscle pain or weakness, dark or reduced urine, new swelling or faintness, jaundice, breathlessness or infusion-site pain requires urgent assessment.</p><p>Follow the urgent contact instructions from your treating hospital. If you cannot reach the team, seek urgent medical assessment. Outside clinic hours, use your instructed out-of-hours pathway or the emergency department.</p></div><p>This page does not diagnose symptoms or decide whether it is safe to stay at home. You do not need to know which medicine caused a symptom before calling.</p></section><section class="pk-section" id="medicines"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Understand your treatment</div><h2>How the medicines work</h2><p class="pk-lead">The aim is to control advanced sarcoma and help symptoms. The strongest trial evidence concerns previously treated liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma; the team explains expected benefit for your particular subtype.</p><div class="pk-grid"><article class="pk-card trabectedin"><div class="pk-type">Chemotherapy</div><h3>Trabectedin</h3><div class="pk-small"></div><svg class="pk-diagram" viewbox="0 0 260 108" aria-hidden="true"><g fill="none" stroke="#086f75" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M35 15c100 20 80 60 190 78M35 93c100-20 80-60 190-78"></path><path d="m53 23 0 61m28-50 0 38m28-26 0 14m28-13 0 12m28-24 0 38m28-50 0 61"></path></g></svg><strong>Disrupts genetic instructions and repair</strong><p>Trabectedin binds to the genetic material, deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), and interferes with how cancer cells use and repair it. This can slow tumour growth; normal blood, liver and muscle cells can also be affected.</p><div class="pk-mechanism"><span>Binds genetic material</span><b aria-hidden="true">↓</b><span>Disrupts transcription and repair</span><b aria-hidden="true">↓</b><span>Blood, liver, muscle and heart checks</span></div><p class="pk-watch"><strong>What to watch for</strong><br>Report fever, marked muscle pain or weakness, dark or reduced urine, jaundice, new swelling, breathlessness or line-site pain urgently.</p><p class="book44-caption">The drawings are simplified explanations of drug action, not anatomical pictures or a prediction of response.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="treatment"><h2>Your treatment</h2><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>The schedule</h3><p>Trabectedin 1.5 mg/m² is infused over 24 hours starting on day 1 of each 21-day cycle. Treatment continues while benefit and tolerability justify it. The actual dose, start time and any delay require a new safety review.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>How it is given</h3><p>Central venous access is strongly recommended. Trained staff connect the prescribed 24-hour infusion and confirm its actual disconnection time. Dexamethasone 20 mg is given intravenously 30 minutes beforehand. The full visit includes checks, preparation and line care.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Other treatment and follow-up</h3><p>A specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team reviews the exact pathology, disease extent, prior treatment and your priorities. Surgery or radiotherapy can have a separate role in selected situations. Symptom control, rehabilitation and palliative care can accompany systemic treatment; they are not restricted to the end of life.</p></article></div></section><section id="evidence" class="pk-section"><h2>Trabectedin versus dacarbazine in previously treated liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma</h2><p>A randomised phase III trial in previously treated liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma found longer progression-free survival with trabectedin than dacarbazine. The final analysis did not demonstrate longer overall survival. This supports a disease-control discussion, not a guarantee that treatment lengthens an individual life.</p><p>The trial population had prior anthracycline and another systemic regimen. It does not establish the same benefit for every soft tissue sarcoma subtype. Survival results from first-line doxorubicin/trabectedin induction followed by maintenance belong to a different treatment strategy.</p></section></section><section class="book44-chapter" id="book44-schedule"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="2"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 02</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Your schedule, tests and appointments</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">Your treatment stages, checks and practical preparations, from before treatment through follow-up.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>NCCP 00374</p></header><section class="pk-section" id="phases"><div class="pk-eyebrow">The bigger treatment plan</div><h2>Your treatment schedule</h2><!--scv43:schedule:start--><section class="scv-schedule-graphic" aria-label="Treatment schedule at a glance"><div class="scv-calendar"><div class="scv-calendar-heading"><strong>Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma</strong><span>21-day view</span></div><div class="scv-calendar-axis"><span>Day</span><div><i style="left:2.381%">1</i><i style="left:35.714%">8</i><i style="left:69.048%">15</i><i style="left:97.619%">21</i></div></div><div class="scv-calendar-row"><div><strong>Trabectedin: 24-hour infusion</strong><span>Day 1–2</span></div><svg viewbox="0 0 600 20" preserveaspectratio="none" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="12" y1="10" x2="588" y2="10" stroke="#cbdedd" stroke-width="1.5"></line><rect x="12.000" y="5" width="54.857" height="10" rx="4" fill="#82cdc7" stroke="#087e80" stroke-width="1"></rect></svg></div></div><p class="scv-graphic-note">This shows treatment timing, not guaranteed symptom-free days. Your team confirms the active phase and dates.</p></section><!--scv43:schedule:end--><div class="pk-phases"><article class="pk-card"><div class="pk-type">Adult advanced soft tissue sarcoma after anthracycline and ifosfamide treatment has failed, or when those medicines are unsuitable</div><h3 class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="infusion" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 4v7M19 11h22v27a11 11 0 0 1-22 0ZM19 21h22M30 49v8h15V42"/><path d="M30 26c-2 4-5 6-5 9a5 5 0 0 0 10 0c0-3-3-5-5-9Z"/></svg></span><span>Trabectedin: one 24-hour infusion every 21 days</span></h3><div class="pk-cycles"><span>Individual prescribed course</span></div><p>Trabectedin 1.5 mg/m² is infused over 24 hours starting on day 1 of each 21-day cycle. Treatment continues while benefit and tolerability justify it. The actual dose, start time and any delay require a new safety review.</p></article></div><p class="pk-small">This is later-line single-agent trabectedin over 24 hours. It is not first-line doxorubicin plus trabectedin with maintenance, and it is not the shorter ovarian-cancer combination infusion. Severe kidney impairment, heart problems or previous serious toxicity require individual oncology/pharmacy review before treatment can continue.</p></section><section class="treatment-journey monitoring-schedule sc44-monitoring-enhanced" id="monitoring-schedule" data-book44-matrix-consolidated="true"><header class="journey-header"><div class="journey-kicker">Baseline → treatment → follow-up</div><h2>Tests and care through treatment</h2></header><p>Safety testing and tumour-response assessment have different purposes. Your plan may change after illness or a dose adjustment.</p><div class="journey-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Tests and care through treatment"></div><p class="journey-footer">The team arranges and reviews tests. This guide shows expected timing; it does not record completed appointments. <a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf">National Cancer Control Programme 00374, version 4a</a></p><!-- sc44-monitoring-details:start --><section class="sc44-monitoring-details" data-sc44-monitoring-details="1" id="named-monitoring-tests"><header class="sc44-section-heading"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg><h2>Tests in your plan</h2></header><div class="sc44-investigation-grid"><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Full blood count</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline and before each cycle. Current product information also specifies weekly testing during the first two cycles, then at least once between doses.</strong></div><p>Detect infection risk, anaemia and bleeding risk.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Liver tests and albumin</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline and before each cycle; liver parameters weekly for the first two cycles, then at least once between doses. Repeat sooner when symptoms or abnormal results require it.</strong></div><p>Check eligibility, liver injury and protein levels relevant to treatment safety.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Kidney function</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline and before cycles; extra tests for reduced urine, dehydration, muscle injury or abnormal results.</strong></div><p>Assess clearance and detect kidney injury, including injury associated with muscle breakdown.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Creatine phosphokinase: muscle-enzyme test</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline, weekly during the first two cycles and at least once between doses thereafter; urgent testing for marked muscle pain, weakness or dark urine.</strong></div><p>Detect muscle injury before it becomes severe.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Heart pumping assessment</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>Heart ultrasound (echocardiogram) or a multigated acquisition heart scan before treatment and every 2–3 months until stopping, as specified in the source complication section. Additional assessment follows symptoms and clinical risk.</strong></div><p>Detect treatment-related heart dysfunction.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Infusion line and pump review</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>Check at connection and disconnection; report pain, swelling, leakage, fever or pump problems immediately.</strong></div><p>Prevent or detect infection, leakage and incorrect infusion delivery.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation sc44-conditional" data-source-ids="nccp"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="imaging" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="7" y="6" width="50" height="47" rx="6"/><path d="M16 15h8m16 0h8M16 44h8m16 0h8M20 20c-4 7-4 13 0 20m24-20c4 7 4 13 0 20"/><path d="M32 17v27m-6-20h12m-12 7h12m-12 7h12M24 59h16"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Tumour response, symptoms and function</h3><span class="sc44-scope">If part of your plan</span><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>The specialist sets scan and clinical-review timing for the tumour type, treatment aim and symptoms.</strong></div><p>Assess benefit separately from safety blood tests.</p></div></article></div></section><!-- sc44-monitoring-details:end --></section><section class="treatment-journey sc44-monitoring-enhanced" id="treatment-journey" data-treatment-journey="00374" data-audience="patient"><header class="journey-header"><div class="journey-kicker">The whole treatment journey</div><h2>Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma: treatment and checks</h2><p class="journey-setting">Adult advanced soft tissue sarcoma after anthracycline and ifosfamide treatment has failed, or when those medicines are unsuitable</p></header><div class="journey-orientation"><strong>Read from left to right.</strong> Columns show stages, not equal lengths of time. This is a general plan: no dates or personal information are needed. Your team confirms which steps apply and arranges appointments.</div><div class="journey-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma: treatment and checks: treatment and monitoring across stages"><table class="journey-table"><caption>Treatment, tests and reviews across the course</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Care / check</th><th scope="col">Before starting</th><th scope="col">During treatment</th><th scope="col">Between visits</th><th scope="col">After treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="journey-monitoring"><th scope="row">Safety tests and symptoms<small><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCCP</a></small></th><td><span>Complete the individual baseline test plan.</span></td><td><span>Repeat the tests at the timing specified in the test cards.</span></td><td><span>Report symptoms promptly; extra checks may be needed.</span></td><td><span>Follow abnormal results and lasting effects until a plan is agreed.</span></td></tr><tr class="journey-monitoring"><th scope="row" class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="medicine" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m17 15 6-6a12 12 0 0 1 17 17l-6 6ZM17 15 7 25a12 12 0 0 0 17 17l10-10M17 15l17 17"/><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="12"/><path d="m37 53 16-16"/></svg></span><span>Medicines and practical care<small><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCCP</a></small></span></th><td><span>Confirm treatment, support and access arrangements.</span></td><td><span>Record actual doses and any safety hold.</span></td><td><span>Follow the written support and contact plan.</span></td><td><span>Agree continuing support and line-care or removal needs.</span></td></tr><tr class="journey-monitoring"><th scope="row" class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span><span>Tumour review and next steps<small><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCCP</a></small></span></th><td><span>Agree the treatment aim and baseline assessment.</span></td><td><span>Review benefit using the specialist plan.</span></td><td><span>Report new or changing tumour-related symptoms.</span></td><td><span>Agree the next treatment or follow-up plan.</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="journey-checks"><h3>What each check is for</h3><details class="journey-milestone" open="open" style=";break-inside:avoid!important"><summary class="sc44-milestone-summary"><span class="journey-number" style=";display:block!important;float:left!important;width:7mm!important;height:7mm!important;line-height:7mm!important;margin:0 2mm 1mm 0!important;text-align:center!important">1</span><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span><span>Treatment review<small>Trabectedin: one 24-hour infusion every 21 days</small></span></summary><div><p>The aim is to control advanced sarcoma and help symptoms. The strongest trial evidence concerns previously treated liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma; the team explains expected benefit for your particular subtype.</p><p><strong>Ask / act:</strong> Call oncology immediately for fever, shaking chills, suddenly feeling unwell, unusual bleeding, new breathlessness, very little urine, severe rash or vomiting that prevents drinking. Follow your hospital’s temperature and contact instructions. Severe breathing difficulty, chest pain, collapse or seizures: call 112/999. Marked muscle pain or weakness, dark or reduced urine, new swelling or faintness, jaundice, breathlessness or infusion-site pain requires urgent assessment.</p></div></details></div><div class="journey-handoff"><h3>At the next review, ask</h3><ul><li>Which treatment phase am I in, and what happens next?</li><li>Which test is expected next, who arranges it, and who explains the result?</li><li>Who should I contact if an expected appointment has not arrived?</li></ul><p>This map does not show whether a test has been ordered, booked or completed. Do not wait for a scheduled test if you develop new symptoms.</p></div></section><section class="access-section" id="port-pump" data-port-pump="00374"><div class="access-kicker">Your treatment, step by step</div><h2>Your port and treatment pump</h2><p class="access-lead">From preparing the line to coming home after disconnection. Your team arranges the appointments and shows you what to do.</p><div class="access-steps"><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 70V35q0-12 12-12h40q12 0 12 12v35M40 24v-9m20 9v-9"></path><circle cx="62" cy="39" r="9"></circle><path d="M62 48v12q0 13-20 13"></path></svg><span>1 · Before treatment</span><h3>Choose central venous access</h3><p>Central access is strongly recommended. The team explains insertion, infection prevention, dressing and flushing.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="20" y="26" width="60" height="48" rx="8"></rect><path d="M35 40h30M35 51h30M35 62h18M50 16v10"></path></svg><span>2 · Before connection</span><h3>Confirm training and contacts</h3><p>Obtain spill/leak instructions, pump guidance and verified daytime/out-of-hours contacts.</p></article><article class="access-step"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="infusion" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 4v7M19 11h22v27a11 11 0 0 1-22 0ZM19 21h22M30 49v8h15V42"/><path d="M30 26c-2 4-5 6-5 9a5 5 0 0 0 10 0c0-3-3-5-5-9Z"/></svg></span><span>3 · Day 1</span><h3>Connect the prescribed infusion</h3><p>Trained staff connect a 24-hour trabectedin infusion after the required premedication.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m14 45 36-29 36 29M25 39v43h50V39M43 82V58h15v24"></path></svg><span>4 · During the next 24 hours</span><h3>Follow the pump and line plan</h3><p>Do not alter settings or disconnect the pump. Report alarms, leakage, pain, swelling or fever promptly.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="50" cy="48" r="30"></circle><path d="M50 27v22l16 10M27 85h46"></path></svg><span>5 · After 24 hours from connection</span><h3>Professional disconnection</h3><p>Attend at the actual prescribed finish time. An apparently empty pump does not authorise self-disconnection.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 23h64v55H18zM32 17v13m36-13v13M18 39h64m-48 18 10 10 21-21"></path></svg><span>6 · After treatment</span><h3>Continue line care</h3><p>Pump disconnection does not remove a port or catheter. Agree ongoing care and any separate removal appointment.</p></article></div><div class="access-details"><details open="open"><summary>Port or peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC): what is the difference?</summary><p>A port is a small chamber beneath the skin, usually on the chest, attached to a tube ending in a large vein. A special needle goes through the skin into the port when it is used. A PICC enters a vein in the upper arm; a short section remains outside under a dressing. Both can deliver a home infusion. Your team helps choose the device and teaches its care.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Before insertion and during recovery</summary><p>Tell the team about allergies, blood thinners, infections and any other cancer medicines. Follow the instructions about eating, drinking and transport; do not stop medicines yourself. Port placement uses local anaesthetic, sometimes with sedation or general anaesthetic. The team explains the procedure and risks, including infection, bleeding, clots and insertion complications. Ask how to manage discomfort, protect the wound and resume activity. Report increasing pain, redness, swelling, discharge or fever.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Connection: what the nurse does</summary><p>The nurse checks the line and treatment, secures the connection and dressing, and starts the pump. Before leaving, you should have the disconnection location and time, contact numbers including out of hours, and the written spill instructions. Ask to be shown what normal pump progress looks like. You do not need to calculate a rate.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Carrying it, sleeping and washing</summary><p>Use the supplied carrier and the position recommended for your pump. Keep tubing free from pulling and kinks. Ask how to place the pump safely for sleep and how to protect the line and dressing when washing. Do not immerse it or swim. Avoid direct heat and cold. An elastomeric pump has a balloon that shrinks slowly and may have no alarm; an electronic pump has different instructions. Follow the instructions for the device you actually receive.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Disconnection is not port removal</summary><p>Keep the appointment even if the pump looks empty, unless the team changes the plan. Staff check the pump and line and arrange safe disposal. After flushing and other line care as required, the port needle may be removed and a small dressing applied. The implanted port stays in place until a separate removal procedure is arranged. A PICC remains in place and needs scheduled dressing and flushing care.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Leakage, disconnection or unexpected pump progress</summary><p>Call the team immediately if the pump leaks, is damaged, finishes unexpectedly early, appears not to run, or the line disconnects. Follow the emergency or spill steps you have been specifically taught, including use of a clamp if instructed. Do not reconnect, change settings or disconnect the pump yourself. Keep other people away from leaked medicine. If medicine touches skin, rinse it off and wash with soap and water, then seek advice.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Pain, swelling or feeling unwell</summary><p>Contact oncology immediately for pain or swelling around the line, a wet/loose dressing, redness, fever, shivering or suddenly feeling unwell. A painful swollen arm or neck needs urgent advice. Severe chest pain, major breathing difficulty or collapse: call 112 / 999. Do not wait for pump disconnection or the next clinic visit.</p></details></div><p class="access-note">The drawings show general steps, not instructions for inserting or accessing a line. Your hospital confirms its own arrangements and the care instructions for your device.</p></section><section id="treatment-logistics" class="access-section" data-treatment-logistics="00374"><div class="access-kicker">Practical treatment guide</div><h2>Practical treatment planning</h2><p class="access-lead">Agree the arrangements with your oncology team.</p><div class="access-steps"><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="18" y="22" width="64" height="60" rx="7"></rect><path d="M18 39h64M33 15v16m34-16v16m-35 30 12 11 23-23"></path></svg><span>STEP 1</span><h3>Before the visit</h3><p>Review all medicines and supplements, especially strong liver-enzyme inhibitors or inducers, statins and other liver-toxic medicines. Avoid alcohol. Confirm steroid premedication, blood tests, pump training, line care, disconnection and verified contact numbers.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="18" y="22" width="64" height="60" rx="7"></rect><path d="M18 39h64M33 15v16m34-16v16m-35 30 12 11 23-23"></path></svg><span>STEP 3</span><h3>Intravenous access and line care</h3><p>The team chooses appropriate intravenous access and explains its care. Report pain, burning, redness or swelling during treatment immediately. If a port or peripherally inserted central catheter is used, confirm dressing, flushing, infection precautions and eventual removal arrangements. Confirm spill or leak instructions and who to call if the pump alarms, becomes disconnected or appears to finish early. Never change settings or disconnect it yourself.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="18" y="22" width="64" height="60" rx="7"></rect><path d="M18 39h64M33 15v16m34-16v16m-35 30 12 11 23-23"></path></svg><span>STEP 4</span><h3>Home support</h3><p>Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before each infusion is required for anti-sickness treatment and appears to help reduce liver toxicity. This regimen has moderate nausea/vomiting risk; agree the complete prevention and rescue prescription without duplicate steroids. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, a white-cell growth factor, may be used for low counts under local policy; it is not universally required.</p></article><article class="access-step"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span><span>STEP 5</span><h3>Next appointment</h3><p>Confirm the next treatment, tests and any scan before leaving.</p></article></div><div class="access-details"></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="checks"><h2>Tests and reviews</h2><p class="pk-lead">Each test has a specific purpose. Use the individual test cards in the monitoring section.</p><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>Your next tests</h3><p>Before leaving, confirm which tests are due, where they will be done and who reviews the results before treatment.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Safety is different from benefit</h3><p>Normal safety blood tests do not show whether the tumour is responding. Your specialist sets the separate scan, symptom and function review.</p></article></div></section></section><section class="book44-chapter" id="book44-care"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="3"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 03</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Symptoms and supportive care</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">Recognising changes, knowing when to call, and using your prescribed supportive-care plan.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>NCCP 00374</p></header><section class="pk-section" id="cycle"><div id="wikiCycleChronology" class="wiki-cycle-map" data-cycle-days="21" style="--cycle-days:21"><h3>Day 1 → Day 21: a general pattern</h3><p class="wiki-cycle-intro">This is a visual orientation, not a prediction of your personal cycle. It shows when some effects often emerge and which effects are <strong>not</strong> tied to a particular day.</p><div class="wiki-cycle-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="General 21-day treatment pattern"><table class="book44-timeline"><thead><tr><th>Treatment or check</th><th>Timing in this view</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-span-start="0" data-span-stop="2"><th>Trabectedin: 24-hour infusion</th><td><svg class="book44-timing-lane" viewbox="0 0 480 42" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Days 1–2"><line x1="12" y1="21" x2="468" y2="21" stroke="#cbdedc" stroke-width="3"></line><rect x="12.0" y="14" width="43.4286" height="14" rx="4" fill="#8acfc8" stroke="#087e80"></rect><text x="12" y="10" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">1</text><text x="468" y="10" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">21</text></svg><strong>Days 1–2</strong><p>Starts on day 1 and continues into day 2, for 24 hours from the actual connection time, not two full days. The team confirms professional disconnection.</p></td></tr><tr data-span-start="0" data-span-stop="21"><th>Checks and symptom reporting</th><td><svg class="book44-timing-lane" viewbox="0 0 480 42" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Days 1–21"><line x1="12" y1="21" x2="468" y2="21" stroke="#cbdedc" stroke-width="3"></line><rect x="12.0" y="14" width="456.0" height="14" rx="4" fill="#8acfc8" stroke="#087e80"></rect><text x="12" y="10" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">1</text><text x="468" y="10" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">21</text></svg><strong>Days 1–21</strong><p>Keep the individual test schedule and report symptoms promptly. Safety checks and tumour-response reviews have different purposes.</p></td></tr><tr data-span-start="0" data-span-stop="1"><th>Dexamethasone premedication</th><td><svg class="book44-timing-lane" viewbox="0 0 480 42" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Day 1"><line x1="12" y1="21" x2="468" y2="21" stroke="#cbdedc" stroke-width="3"></line><rect x="12.0" y="14" width="21.7143" height="14" rx="4" fill="#8acfc8" stroke="#087e80"></rect><text x="12" y="10" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">1</text><text x="468" y="10" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">21</text></svg><strong>Day 1</strong><p>Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before trabectedin; reconcile the complete anti-sickness prescription.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="wiki-cycle-key"><div><strong>Schedule scope:</strong> This is later-line single-agent trabectedin over 24 hours. It is not first-line doxorubicin plus trabectedin with maintenance, and it is not the shorter ovarian-cancer combination infusion. Severe kidney impairment, heart problems or previous serious toxicity require individual oncology/pharmacy review before treatment can continue.</div><div><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Symptoms can occur on any day. An unshaded day is not a guarantee that treatment effects have passed.</div></div><div class="wiki-cycle-evidence"><strong>Why these bands stay broad:</strong> Illustrative treatment days, not dated appointments or guaranteed safe days. Your team confirms the actual doses, delays, support and appointments. Band length does not show likelihood or severity, and the chart cannot tell you whether it is safe to receive treatment. <a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Cancer Control Programme 00374, version 4a ↗</a></div><p class="wiki-cycle-warning"><strong>There are no guaranteed “safe days”.</strong> Follow your own prescription and hospital instructions. This information supports a discussion; it does not authorise treatment.</p></div><details open="open"><summary>Between-treatment notes</summary><h3>Day 1–2: Trabectedin: 24-hour infusion</h3><p>Starts on day 1 and continues into day 2, for 24 hours from the actual connection time, not two full days. The team confirms professional disconnection. Follow your confirmed appointment and support plan.</p><h3>Day 3–21: Between scheduled chemotherapy doses</h3><p>Continue the prescribed support and monitoring plan. Side effects can occur between visits; contact oncology promptly if unwell. Follow your confirmed appointment and support plan.</p></details></section><section class="pk-section" id="support"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Make the plan practical</div><h2>Support around the medicines</h2><div class="pk-two pk-support"><article class="pk-card"><h3 class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="medicine" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m17 15 6-6a12 12 0 0 1 17 17l-6 6ZM17 15 7 25a12 12 0 0 0 17 17l10-10M17 15l17 17"/><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="12"/><path d="m37 53 16-16"/></svg></span><span>Preparation and support medicines</span></h3><p>Review all medicines and supplements, especially strong liver-enzyme inhibitors or inducers, statins and other liver-toxic medicines. Avoid alcohol. Confirm steroid premedication, blood tests, pump training, line care, disconnection and verified contact numbers. Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before each infusion is required for anti-sickness treatment and appears to help reduce liver toxicity. This regimen has moderate nausea/vomiting risk; agree the complete prevention and rescue prescription without duplicate steroids. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, a white-cell growth factor, may be used for low counts under local policy; it is not universally required.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Fertility and pregnancy precautions</h3><p>Discuss fertility preservation before treatment. Avoid pregnancy and obtain written contraception and breastfeeding instructions for every medicine, including how long precautions continue after the last dose. Do not assume all drugs have the same waiting period. Contact oncology promptly if pregnancy is possible.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="support"><h2>Support and daily life</h2><p class="pk-lead">Follow the written plan agreed with your team.</p><div class="pk-two"></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="urgent"><h2>Symptoms and help</h2><p class="pk-lead">Report new or worsening symptoms promptly.</p><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>During a drip</h3><p>Tell the nurse immediately about flushing, rash, chills, breathlessness, faintness or pain/swelling at the infusion site.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Your contacts</h3><p>Keep verified daytime and out-of-hours oncology numbers with your treatment card. The quick response code website is information, not an emergency service.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="consent"><h2>Your treatment decision</h2><p class="pk-lead">Read the general chemotherapy risks and the individual medicine risks together.</p><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>General chemotherapy risks</h3><p>Discuss low blood counts with serious infection or sepsis, anaemia and bleeding; nausea or vomiting; mouth soreness, appetite or taste changes, diarrhoea or constipation; fatigue, hair, nail and skin changes; changes in sexual wellbeing; allergic reactions and intravenous-line complications; blood clots, including clots in the legs or lungs; fertility, pregnancy and breastfeeding effects; and the possibility of lasting organ injury, rare later cancers or death from serious treatment complications. The likelihood and severity differ between medicines and people. Use this general discussion together with every medicine-specific risk below.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Your priorities and alternatives</h3><p>Discuss realistic benefit, uncertainty, alternatives including observation or symptom-focused care when appropriate, serious and lasting risks, personal priorities and the option to decline or reconsider treatment. This is later-line single-agent trabectedin over 24 hours. It is not first-line doxorubicin plus trabectedin with maintenance, and it is not the shorter ovarian-cancer combination infusion. Severe kidney impairment, heart problems or previous serious toxicity require individual oncology/pharmacy review before treatment can continue.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-faq" id="questions"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Prepare for the conversation</div><h2>Questions worth asking</h2><details open="open"><summary>What is the treatment trying to do?</summary><p>The aim is to control advanced sarcoma and help symptoms. The strongest trial evidence concerns previously treated liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma; the team explains expected benefit for your particular subtype. A specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team reviews the exact pathology, disease extent, prior treatment and your priorities. Surgery or radiotherapy can have a separate role in selected situations. Symptom control, rehabilitation and palliative care can accompany systemic treatment; they are not restricted to the end of life.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Does a delay mean treatment has failed?</summary><p>No. Blood counts, organ function and symptoms can make a delay or dose change safer. Your team decides whether and when to give the next dose.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Who do I contact if unwell?</summary><p>Use your verified oncology daytime or out-of-hours number. If you cannot reach the team, seek urgent medical assessment. Severe symptoms require 112/999.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Can I restart after a delay or safety hold?</summary><p>Only after the treating team gives an explicit plan. A normal result does not erase a previous severe reaction or permanent-stop decision.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Are safety tests the same as checking benefit?</summary><p>No. Safety tests look for treatment effects on the body. Scans, symptoms and function help the specialist assess tumour control.</p></details><div class="pk-note"><strong>Try explaining the plan back:</strong> Which medicines are given on each treatment day?,Which symptoms mean you should call immediately?,When are your next tests and review?</div></section><section class="book44-phase" data-support-source="support-day1.html"><div class="notice"><strong>Supportive prescription draft for review.</strong> This is a regimen template. It is not an issued patient prescription; individual review and completion are required.</div><h2>Day 1: trabectedin</h2><p><strong>Active medicines:</strong> Trabectedin</p><p><strong>Emetogenic risk:</strong> moderate</p><div class="support-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Supportive medicines: medicine, dose and timing"><table><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Dose / route</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dexamethasone</td><td>20 mg intravenously</td><td>30 minutes before every trabectedin infusion<small>Required anti-sickness premedication that appears to help reduce liver toxicity. Do not add the generic 8 mg steroid reference on top; reconcile all support steroids.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Individual anti-sickness prescription</td><td>Prescribed by the oncology team</td><td>The team specifies prevention, delayed-sickness and rescue timing<small>Reconcile interactions and the actual support prescription; this reference does not authorise self-treatment.</small></td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class="local-support-reference"><h3>Supplied local prescription reference</h3><p><strong>CUH Department of Medical Oncology: Adjuvant medication prescription for chemotherapy Moderate Risk for nausea and vomiting, Version 2 (05/05/2021)</strong></p><p>The local CUH source is retained as a reference list, not as the national antiemetic standard. Nystatin/chlorhexidine are displayed as local mouth-care adjuncts, not universal antiemetic or mucositis prophylaxis. Verify current local oral-care policy. This historical local proforma needs current pharmacy confirmation. It is an alternative reference, not a second course to add automatically.</p><div class="support-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Supportive medicines: medicine, dose and timing"><table><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Dose / route / frequency</th><th>Timing and directions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dexamethasone</td><td>8 mg by mouth once daily</td><td>Days 2-3<small>Take once daily for two days starting the day after chemotherapy.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Omeprazole</td><td>20 mg by mouth once daily</td><td>Days 1-3<small>Take one capsule daily for three days starting on the day of chemotherapy.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Nystatin</td><td>100,000 units/mL, 1 mL by mouth four times daily</td><td>Continuous during chemotherapy<small>Rinse 1 mL around the mouth four times daily after meals during the chemotherapy regimen.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Chlorhexidine mouthwash</td><td>10 mL by mouth four times daily</td><td>Continuous during chemotherapy<small>Rinse 10 mL around the mouth for at least 1 minute four times daily; expel after rinsing.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Cyclizine</td><td>50 mg by mouth three times daily as required</td><td>As required during chemotherapy<small>Take one tablet three times daily as required.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Metoclopramide</td><td>10 mg by mouth three times daily as required</td><td>As required during chemotherapy<small>Take one tablet three times daily as required.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Loperamide</td><td>4 mg once initially, then 2 mg by mouth four times daily as required</td><td>Chemotherapy-induced diarrhoea; max 16 mg/day<small>Take 4 mg after the first episode of diarrhoea, then 2 mg after each further episode, to a maximum of 16 mg/day.</small><small class="local-source-clarification">Source clarification: the supplied CUH reference gives both four-times-daily as-needed and after-each-episode dosing. Oncology/pharmacy must confirm one schedule before use. Follow the individual prescription; 16 mg/day is a maximum, not a target.</small></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><h3>Additional phase-specific support</h3><ul><li>Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before each infusion is required for anti-sickness treatment and appears to help reduce liver toxicity. This regimen has moderate nausea/vomiting risk; agree the complete prevention and rescue prescription without duplicate steroids. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, a white-cell growth factor, may be used for low counts under local policy; it is not universally required.</li><li>Review all medicines and supplements, especially strong liver-enzyme inhibitors or inducers, statins and other liver-toxic medicines. Avoid alcohol. Confirm steroid premedication, blood tests, pump training, line care, disconnection and verified contact numbers.</li><li>The team chooses appropriate intravenous access and explains its care. Report pain, burning, redness or swelling during treatment immediately. If a port or peripherally inserted central catheter is used, confirm dressing, flushing, infection precautions and eventual removal arrangements. Confirm spill or leak instructions and who to call if the pump alarms, becomes disconnected or appears to finish early. Never change settings or disconnect it yourself.</li><li>An improved test result does not authorise restarting after a safety hold; confirm the new prescription.</li><li>Olanzapine may be added for breakthrough nausea/vomiting; cyclizine may be considered where the patient is not already receiving olanzapine.</li><li>Only optional additional antiemetic steroid may be adjusted after prescription reconciliation. Retain the required dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before every trabectedin infusion; the generic omission advice does not apply to this required premedication.</li><li>Metoclopramide: adult maximum 10 mg up to three times daily (30 mg/day) and maximum treatment duration 5 days under current European Medicines Agency/Irish product information; avoid prolonged repeated use without review.</li></ul><p><strong>Before signing:</strong> verify allergies, current medicines, interactions, renal / hepatic function, QT risk, sedation, prior response, total steroid exposure and the rescue-medicine duration. Do not duplicate medicines from another phase.</p><p>Individual changes / rescue duration: __________________________________<br>Prescriber / date: __________________ Pharmacy review: __________________</p></section></section><section class="book44-reference-anatomy" id="anatomical-effects" data-book44-anatomy-source="index.html"><h2>Your anatomical side-effect map</h2><section class="patient-bodymap-card hcc-bodymap regimen-anatomy" id="side-effects" data-authored-map data-regimen-map="00374"><div class="hcc-visual-title"><h4>A picture of side effects</h4><p>Match each symptom box to its numbered body location. Tell your team about new symptoms.</p></div><div class="hcc-visual-stage"><svg class="hcc-static-connectors" aria-hidden="true" viewbox="0 0 100 100" preserveaspectratio="none"><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="12.5" x2="50" y2="11.8625"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="12.5" x2="49.28" y2="22.8125"></line><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="37.5" x2="47.48" y2="30.1125"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="37.5" x2="46.22" y2="35.2225"></line><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="62.5" x2="55.04" y2="50.1875"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="62.5" x2="50" y2="40.6063"></line><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="87.5" x2="55.4" y2="63.8750"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="87.5" x2="44.6" y2="70.2625"></line></svg><div class="hcc-organ-column"><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="mouth"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">1</span><strong>Mouth and appetite</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report mouth ulcers, soreness or trouble drinking early. Use the prescribed mouth-care and anti-sickness plan.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="liver"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">3</span><strong>Liver</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Blood tests detect liver effects. Yellow eyes, dark urine or new significant upper abdominal pain needs prompt assessment.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="blood"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">5</span><strong>Blood cells</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Low counts can cause infection, anaemia or bleeding. Fever, shaking chills or sudden illness needs immediate oncology contact.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="muscle"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">7</span><strong>Muscles</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>New marked muscle pain or weakness needs urgent review. Blood tests check for muscle injury even without symptoms.</p></article></div><div class="hcc-anatomy"><div class="hcc-image-wrap"><img src="../../assets/patient/anatomy-hcc-v0762.png" alt="Full-body anatomical illustration with numbered symptom locations"><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="mouth" style="left:50%;top:13%"><span>1</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="heart" style="left:48%;top:25%"><span>2</span></span><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="liver" style="left:43%;top:33%"><span>3</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="kidneys" style="left:39.5%;top:38.6%"><span>4</span></span><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="blood" style="left:64%;top:55%"><span>5</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="bowel" style="left:50%;top:44.5%"><span>6</span></span><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="muscle" style="left:65%;top:70%"><span>7</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="whole" style="left:35%;top:77%"><span>8</span></span></div></div><div class="hcc-organ-column"><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="heart"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">2</span><strong>Heart and fluid leakage</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report new breathlessness, ankle swelling, faintness or rapid weight gain urgently. Heart tests continue during treatment.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="kidneys"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">4</span><strong>Kidneys and urine</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Muscle breakdown can damage kidneys. Dark or reduced urine, especially with muscle pain, needs urgent assessment.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="bowel"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">6</span><strong>Bowel</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report persistent diarrhoea or constipation, severe pain, abdominal swelling or inability to drink.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="whole"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">8</span><strong>Infusion line and daily life</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report line-site pain, burning or swelling immediately. Keep the pump and line-care plan; do not alter settings yourself.</p></article></div></div><div class="hcc-visual-foot">Selected effects, not every possible effect or a prediction. Blood-count, skin and line markers are schematic. Number and text carry meaning; colour is decorative.</div></section></section><section class="book44-chapter book44-sources"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="4"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 04</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Sources and further information</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">The references behind this information and addresses you can use to find further help.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>National Cancer Control Programme (NCCP) 00374</p></header><p>Use this information alongside the plan agreed with your treatment team. Your team confirms your medicines, doses and appointments.</p><ol class="book44-refs"><li><span class="book44-source-label">NCCP 00374 v4a · pTreatment, monitoring, administration and supportive-care sections</span><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf">https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">eviQ: port</span><a href="https://www.eviq.org.au/p/507">https://www.eviq.org.au/p/507</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">eviQ: peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC)</span><a href="https://www.eviq.org.au/p/543">https://www.eviq.org.au/p/543</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">eviQ: pump</span><a href="https://www.eviq.org.au/p/502">https://www.eviq.org.au/p/502</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">Trabectedin versus dacarbazine in previously treated liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma</span><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5070559/">https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5070559/</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">European Medicines Agency: Yondelis product information</span><a href="https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/product-information/yondelis-epar-product-information_en.pdf">https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/product-information/yondelis-epar-product-information_en.pdf</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">Final overall-survival analysis of trabectedin versus dacarbazine</span><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31173362/">https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31173362/</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">National Cancer Institute: soft tissue sarcoma treatment context</span><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/types/soft-tissue-sarcoma/hp/adult-soft-tissue-treatment-pdq">https://www.cancer.gov/types/soft-tissue-sarcoma/hp/adult-soft-tissue-treatment-pdq</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">National Cancer Institute: palliative and supportive care alongside cancer treatment</span><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet">https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet</a></li></ol></section></main></body></html>+</style><style data-v54-caption-keep>.book44 .journey-table caption{break-after:avoid!important;page-break-after:avoid!important}.book44 .journey-table thead{break-before:avoid!important;page-break-before:avoid!important}</style><!--scv43:stylesheet:start--><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../assets/patient/patient-visual-standard-v08443.css"><!--scv43:stylesheet:end--><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../assets/patient/patient-visual-language-v08444.css" data-sc44-visual-language="1"></head><body data-sact-editorial-v44="0.84.44" class="book44" data-patient-visual-standard="v08443"><main><section class="book44-chapter" id="book44-treatment"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="1"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 01</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Understanding your treatment</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">Why this treatment is offered, what its medicines do, and how the agreed plan fits your care.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>National Cancer Control Programme (NCCP) 00374</p></header><section class="pk-section" id="urgent"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Act on symptoms, not the calendar</div><h2>When to get help</h2><div class="pk-note urgent"><h3>Emergency: call 112 / 999</h3><p>Severe breathing difficulty, collapse, seizure or severe chest pain: call emergency services. Do not wait for an online answer.</p></div><div class="pk-note urgent"><h3>Contact your oncology service immediately</h3><p>Temperature 37.5°C or higher, 36°C or lower, shivering or suddenly feeling unwell — even with a normal temperature. Call oncology immediately for fever, shaking chills, suddenly feeling unwell, unusual bleeding, new breathlessness, very little urine, severe rash or vomiting that prevents drinking. Follow your hospital’s temperature and contact instructions. Severe breathing difficulty, chest pain, collapse or seizures: call 112/999. Marked muscle pain or weakness, dark or reduced urine, new swelling or faintness, jaundice, breathlessness or infusion-site pain requires urgent assessment.</p><p>Follow the urgent contact instructions from your treating hospital. If you cannot reach the team, seek urgent medical assessment. Outside clinic hours, use your instructed out-of-hours pathway or the emergency department.</p></div><p>This page does not diagnose symptoms or decide whether it is safe to stay at home. You do not need to know which medicine caused a symptom before calling.</p></section><section class="pk-section" id="medicines"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Understand your treatment</div><h2>How the medicines work</h2><p class="pk-lead">The aim is to control advanced sarcoma and help symptoms. The strongest trial evidence concerns previously treated liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma; the team explains expected benefit for your particular subtype.</p><div class="pk-grid"><article class="pk-card trabectedin"><div class="pk-type">Chemotherapy</div><h3>Trabectedin</h3><div class="pk-small"></div><svg class="pk-diagram" viewbox="0 0 260 108" aria-hidden="true"><g fill="none" stroke="#086f75" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M35 15c100 20 80 60 190 78M35 93c100-20 80-60 190-78"></path><path d="m53 23 0 61m28-50 0 38m28-26 0 14m28-13 0 12m28-24 0 38m28-50 0 61"></path></g></svg><strong>Disrupts genetic instructions and repair</strong><p>Trabectedin binds to the genetic material, deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), and interferes with how cancer cells use and repair it. This can slow tumour growth; normal blood, liver and muscle cells can also be affected.</p><div class="pk-mechanism"><span>Binds genetic material</span><b aria-hidden="true">↓</b><span>Disrupts transcription and repair</span><b aria-hidden="true">↓</b><span>Blood, liver, muscle and heart checks</span></div><p class="pk-watch"><strong>What to watch for</strong><br>Report fever, marked muscle pain or weakness, dark or reduced urine, jaundice, new swelling, breathlessness or line-site pain urgently.</p><p class="book44-caption">The drawings are simplified explanations of drug action, not anatomical pictures or a prediction of response.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="treatment"><h2>Your treatment</h2><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>The schedule</h3><p>Trabectedin 1.5 mg/m² is infused over 24 hours starting on day 1 of each 21-day cycle. Treatment continues while benefit and tolerability justify it. The actual dose, start time and any delay require a new safety review.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>How it is given</h3><p>Central venous access is strongly recommended. Trained staff connect the prescribed 24-hour infusion and confirm its actual disconnection time. Dexamethasone 20 mg is given intravenously 30 minutes beforehand. The full visit includes checks, preparation and line care.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Other treatment and follow-up</h3><p>A specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team reviews the exact pathology, disease extent, prior treatment and your priorities. Surgery or radiotherapy can have a separate role in selected situations. Symptom control, rehabilitation and palliative care can accompany systemic treatment; they are not restricted to the end of life.</p></article></div></section><section id="evidence" class="pk-section"><h2>Trabectedin versus dacarbazine in previously treated liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma</h2><p>A randomised phase III trial in previously treated liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma found longer progression-free survival with trabectedin than dacarbazine. The final analysis did not demonstrate longer overall survival. This supports a disease-control discussion, not a guarantee that treatment lengthens an individual life.</p><p>The trial population had prior anthracycline and another systemic regimen. It does not establish the same benefit for every soft tissue sarcoma subtype. Survival results from first-line doxorubicin/trabectedin induction followed by maintenance belong to a different treatment strategy.</p></section></section><section class="book44-chapter" id="book44-schedule"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="2"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 02</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Your schedule, tests and appointments</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">Your treatment stages, checks and practical preparations, from before treatment through follow-up.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>NCCP 00374</p></header><section class="pk-section" id="phases"><div class="pk-eyebrow">The bigger treatment plan</div><h2>Your treatment schedule</h2><!--scv43:schedule:start--><section class="scv-schedule-graphic" aria-label="Treatment schedule at a glance"><div class="scv-calendar"><div class="scv-calendar-heading"><strong>Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma</strong><span>21-day view</span></div><div class="scv-calendar-axis"><span>Day</span><div><i style="left:2.381%">1</i><i style="left:35.714%">8</i><i style="left:69.048%">15</i><i style="left:97.619%">21</i></div></div><div class="scv-calendar-row"><div><strong>Trabectedin: 24-hour infusion</strong><span>Day 1–2</span></div><svg viewBox="0 0 600 20" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="12" y1="10" x2="588" y2="10" stroke="#cbdedd" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="12.000" y="5" width="54.857" height="10" rx="4" fill="#82cdc7" stroke="#087e80" stroke-width="1"/></svg></div></div><p class="scv-graphic-note">This shows treatment timing, not guaranteed symptom-free days. Your team confirms the active phase and dates.</p></section><!--scv43:schedule:end--><div class="pk-phases"><article class="pk-card"><div class="pk-type">Adult advanced soft tissue sarcoma after anthracycline and ifosfamide treatment has failed, or when those medicines are unsuitable</div><h3 class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="infusion" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 4v7M19 11h22v27a11 11 0 0 1-22 0ZM19 21h22M30 49v8h15V42"/><path d="M30 26c-2 4-5 6-5 9a5 5 0 0 0 10 0c0-3-3-5-5-9Z"/></svg></span><span>Trabectedin: one 24-hour infusion every 21 days</span></h3><div class="pk-cycles"><span>Individual prescribed course</span></div><p>Trabectedin 1.5 mg/m² is infused over 24 hours starting on day 1 of each 21-day cycle. Treatment continues while benefit and tolerability justify it. The actual dose, start time and any delay require a new safety review.</p></article></div><p class="pk-small">This is later-line single-agent trabectedin over 24 hours. It is not first-line doxorubicin plus trabectedin with maintenance, and it is not the shorter ovarian-cancer combination infusion. Severe kidney impairment, heart problems or previous serious toxicity require individual oncology/pharmacy review before treatment can continue.</p></section><section class="treatment-journey monitoring-schedule sc44-monitoring-enhanced" id="monitoring-schedule" data-book44-matrix-consolidated="true"><header class="journey-header"><div class="journey-kicker">Baseline → treatment → follow-up</div><h2>Tests and care through treatment</h2></header><p>Safety testing and tumour-response assessment have different purposes. Your plan may change after illness or a dose adjustment.</p><div class="journey-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Tests and care through treatment"></div><p class="journey-footer">The team arranges and reviews tests. This guide shows expected timing; it does not record completed appointments. <a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf">National Cancer Control Programme 00374, version 4a</a></p><!-- sc44-monitoring-details:start --><section class="sc44-monitoring-details" data-sc44-monitoring-details="1" id="named-monitoring-tests"><header class="sc44-section-heading"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg><h2>Tests in your plan</h2></header><div class="sc44-investigation-grid"><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Full blood count</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline and before each cycle. Current product information also specifies weekly testing during the first two cycles, then at least once between doses.</strong></div><p>Detect infection risk, anaemia and bleeding risk.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Liver tests and albumin</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline and before each cycle; liver parameters weekly for the first two cycles, then at least once between doses. Repeat sooner when symptoms or abnormal results require it.</strong></div><p>Check eligibility, liver injury and protein levels relevant to treatment safety.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Kidney function</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline and before cycles; extra tests for reduced urine, dehydration, muscle injury or abnormal results.</strong></div><p>Assess clearance and detect kidney injury, including injury associated with muscle breakdown.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="blood" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7h14v7H9zM11 14v35a5 5 0 0 0 10 0V14M11 34h10M30 7h14v7H30zM32 14v21M42 14v14"/><path d="M47 27c-3 6-9 12-9 18a9 9 0 0 0 18 0c0-6-6-12-9-18Z"/><path d="M43 46c0 3 2 4 4 4"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Creatine phosphokinase: muscle-enzyme test</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>At baseline, weekly during the first two cycles and at least once between doses thereafter; urgent testing for marked muscle pain, weakness or dark urine.</strong></div><p>Detect muscle injury before it becomes severe.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Heart pumping assessment</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>Heart ultrasound (echocardiogram) or a multigated acquisition heart scan before treatment and every 2–3 months until stopping, as specified in the source complication section. Additional assessment follows symptoms and clinical risk.</strong></div><p>Detect treatment-related heart dysfunction.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation" data-source-ids="nccp trabectedin-pi"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Infusion line and pump review</h3><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>Check at connection and disconnection; report pain, swelling, leakage, fever or pump problems immediately.</strong></div><p>Prevent or detect infection, leakage and incorrect infusion delivery.</p></div></article><article class="sc44-investigation sc44-conditional" data-source-ids="nccp"><div class="sc44-investigation-symbol"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="imaging" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="7" y="6" width="50" height="47" rx="6"/><path d="M16 15h8m16 0h8M16 44h8m16 0h8M20 20c-4 7-4 13 0 20m24-20c4 7 4 13 0 20"/><path d="M32 17v27m-6-20h12m-12 7h12m-12 7h12M24 59h16"/></svg></span></div><div class="sc44-investigation-content"><h3>Tumour response, symptoms and function</h3><span class="sc44-scope">If part of your plan</span><div class="sc44-investigation-time"><span>When</span><strong>The specialist sets scan and clinical-review timing for the tumour type, treatment aim and symptoms.</strong></div><p>Assess benefit separately from safety blood tests.</p></div></article></div></section><!-- sc44-monitoring-details:end --></section><section class="treatment-journey sc44-monitoring-enhanced" id="treatment-journey" data-treatment-journey="00374" data-audience="patient"><header class="journey-header"><div class="journey-kicker">The whole treatment journey</div><h2>Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma: treatment and checks</h2><p class="journey-setting">Adult advanced soft tissue sarcoma after anthracycline and ifosfamide treatment has failed, or when those medicines are unsuitable</p></header><div class="journey-orientation"><strong>Read from left to right.</strong> Columns show stages, not equal lengths of time. This is a general plan: no dates or personal information are needed. Your team confirms which steps apply and arranges appointments.</div><div class="journey-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma: treatment and checks: treatment and monitoring across stages"><table class="journey-table"><caption>Treatment, tests and reviews across the course</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Care / check</th><th scope="col">Before starting</th><th scope="col">During treatment</th><th scope="col">Between visits</th><th scope="col">After treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="journey-monitoring"><th scope="row">Safety tests and symptoms<small><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCCP</a></small></th><td><span>Complete the individual baseline test plan.</span></td><td><span>Repeat the tests at the timing specified in the test cards.</span></td><td><span>Report symptoms promptly; extra checks may be needed.</span></td><td><span>Follow abnormal results and lasting effects until a plan is agreed.</span></td></tr><tr class="journey-monitoring"><th scope="row" class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="medicine" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m17 15 6-6a12 12 0 0 1 17 17l-6 6ZM17 15 7 25a12 12 0 0 0 17 17l10-10M17 15l17 17"/><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="12"/><path d="m37 53 16-16"/></svg></span><span>Medicines and practical care<small><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCCP</a></small></span></th><td><span>Confirm treatment, support and access arrangements.</span></td><td><span>Record actual doses and any safety hold.</span></td><td><span>Follow the written support and contact plan.</span></td><td><span>Agree continuing support and line-care or removal needs.</span></td></tr><tr class="journey-monitoring"><th scope="row" class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span><span>Tumour review and next steps<small><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCCP</a></small></span></th><td><span>Agree the treatment aim and baseline assessment.</span></td><td><span>Review benefit using the specialist plan.</span></td><td><span>Report new or changing tumour-related symptoms.</span></td><td><span>Agree the next treatment or follow-up plan.</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="journey-checks"><h3>What each check is for</h3><details class="journey-milestone" open="open" style=";break-inside:avoid!important"><summary class="sc44-milestone-summary"><span class="journey-number" style=";display:block!important;float:left!important;width:7mm!important;height:7mm!important;line-height:7mm!important;margin:0 2mm 1mm 0!important;text-align:center!important">1</span><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span><span>Treatment review<small>Trabectedin: one 24-hour infusion every 21 days</small></span></summary><div><p>The aim is to control advanced sarcoma and help symptoms. The strongest trial evidence concerns previously treated liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma; the team explains expected benefit for your particular subtype.</p><p><strong>Ask / act:</strong> Call oncology immediately for fever, shaking chills, suddenly feeling unwell, unusual bleeding, new breathlessness, very little urine, severe rash or vomiting that prevents drinking. Follow your hospital’s temperature and contact instructions. Severe breathing difficulty, chest pain, collapse or seizures: call 112/999. Marked muscle pain or weakness, dark or reduced urine, new swelling or faintness, jaundice, breathlessness or infusion-site pain requires urgent assessment.</p></div></details></div><div class="journey-handoff"><h3>At the next review, ask</h3><ul><li>Which treatment phase am I in, and what happens next?</li><li>Which test is expected next, who arranges it, and who explains the result?</li><li>Who should I contact if an expected appointment has not arrived?</li></ul><p>This map does not show whether a test has been ordered, booked or completed. Do not wait for a scheduled test if you develop new symptoms.</p></div></section><section class="access-section" id="port-pump" data-port-pump="00374"><div class="access-kicker">Your treatment, step by step</div><h2>Your port and treatment pump</h2><p class="access-lead">From preparing the line to coming home after disconnection. Your team arranges the appointments and shows you what to do.</p><div class="access-steps"><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 70V35q0-12 12-12h40q12 0 12 12v35M40 24v-9m20 9v-9"></path><circle cx="62" cy="39" r="9"></circle><path d="M62 48v12q0 13-20 13"></path></svg><span>1 · Before treatment</span><h3>Choose central venous access</h3><p>Central access is strongly recommended. The team explains insertion, infection prevention, dressing and flushing.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="20" y="26" width="60" height="48" rx="8"></rect><path d="M35 40h30M35 51h30M35 62h18M50 16v10"></path></svg><span>2 · Before connection</span><h3>Confirm training and contacts</h3><p>Obtain spill/leak instructions, pump guidance and verified daytime/out-of-hours contacts.</p></article><article class="access-step"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="infusion" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 4v7M19 11h22v27a11 11 0 0 1-22 0ZM19 21h22M30 49v8h15V42"/><path d="M30 26c-2 4-5 6-5 9a5 5 0 0 0 10 0c0-3-3-5-5-9Z"/></svg></span><span>3 · Day 1</span><h3>Connect the prescribed infusion</h3><p>Trained staff connect a 24-hour trabectedin infusion after the required premedication.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m14 45 36-29 36 29M25 39v43h50V39M43 82V58h15v24"></path></svg><span>4 · During the next 24 hours</span><h3>Follow the pump and line plan</h3><p>Do not alter settings or disconnect the pump. Report alarms, leakage, pain, swelling or fever promptly.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="50" cy="48" r="30"></circle><path d="M50 27v22l16 10M27 85h46"></path></svg><span>5 · After 24 hours from connection</span><h3>Professional disconnection</h3><p>Attend at the actual prescribed finish time. An apparently empty pump does not authorise self-disconnection.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 23h64v55H18zM32 17v13m36-13v13M18 39h64m-48 18 10 10 21-21"></path></svg><span>6 · After treatment</span><h3>Continue line care</h3><p>Pump disconnection does not remove a port or catheter. Agree ongoing care and any separate removal appointment.</p></article></div><div class="access-details"><details open="open"><summary>Port or peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC): what is the difference?</summary><p>A port is a small chamber beneath the skin, usually on the chest, attached to a tube ending in a large vein. A special needle goes through the skin into the port when it is used. A PICC enters a vein in the upper arm; a short section remains outside under a dressing. Both can deliver a home infusion. Your team helps choose the device and teaches its care.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Before insertion and during recovery</summary><p>Tell the team about allergies, blood thinners, infections and any other cancer medicines. Follow the instructions about eating, drinking and transport; do not stop medicines yourself. Port placement uses local anaesthetic, sometimes with sedation or general anaesthetic. The team explains the procedure and risks, including infection, bleeding, clots and insertion complications. Ask how to manage discomfort, protect the wound and resume activity. Report increasing pain, redness, swelling, discharge or fever.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Connection: what the nurse does</summary><p>The nurse checks the line and treatment, secures the connection and dressing, and starts the pump. Before leaving, you should have the disconnection location and time, contact numbers including out of hours, and the written spill instructions. Ask to be shown what normal pump progress looks like. You do not need to calculate a rate.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Carrying it, sleeping and washing</summary><p>Use the supplied carrier and the position recommended for your pump. Keep tubing free from pulling and kinks. Ask how to place the pump safely for sleep and how to protect the line and dressing when washing. Do not immerse it or swim. Avoid direct heat and cold. An elastomeric pump has a balloon that shrinks slowly and may have no alarm; an electronic pump has different instructions. Follow the instructions for the device you actually receive.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Disconnection is not port removal</summary><p>Keep the appointment even if the pump looks empty, unless the team changes the plan. Staff check the pump and line and arrange safe disposal. After flushing and other line care as required, the port needle may be removed and a small dressing applied. The implanted port stays in place until a separate removal procedure is arranged. A PICC remains in place and needs scheduled dressing and flushing care.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Leakage, disconnection or unexpected pump progress</summary><p>Call the team immediately if the pump leaks, is damaged, finishes unexpectedly early, appears not to run, or the line disconnects. Follow the emergency or spill steps you have been specifically taught, including use of a clamp if instructed. Do not reconnect, change settings or disconnect the pump yourself. Keep other people away from leaked medicine. If medicine touches skin, rinse it off and wash with soap and water, then seek advice.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Pain, swelling or feeling unwell</summary><p>Contact oncology immediately for pain or swelling around the line, a wet/loose dressing, redness, fever, shivering or suddenly feeling unwell. A painful swollen arm or neck needs urgent advice. Severe chest pain, major breathing difficulty or collapse: call 112 / 999. Do not wait for pump disconnection or the next clinic visit.</p></details></div><p class="access-note">The drawings show general steps, not instructions for inserting or accessing a line. Your hospital confirms its own arrangements and the care instructions for your device.</p></section><section id="treatment-logistics" class="access-section" data-treatment-logistics="00374"><div class="access-kicker">Practical treatment guide</div><h2>Practical treatment planning</h2><p class="access-lead">Agree the arrangements with your oncology team.</p><div class="access-steps"><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="18" y="22" width="64" height="60" rx="7"></rect><path d="M18 39h64M33 15v16m34-16v16m-35 30 12 11 23-23"></path></svg><span>STEP 1</span><h3>Before the visit</h3><p>Review all medicines and supplements, especially strong liver-enzyme inhibitors or inducers, statins and other liver-toxic medicines. Avoid alcohol. Confirm steroid premedication, blood tests, pump training, line care, disconnection and verified contact numbers.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="18" y="22" width="64" height="60" rx="7"></rect><path d="M18 39h64M33 15v16m34-16v16m-35 30 12 11 23-23"></path></svg><span>STEP 3</span><h3>Intravenous access and line care</h3><p>The team chooses appropriate intravenous access and explains its care. Report pain, burning, redness or swelling during treatment immediately. If a port or peripherally inserted central catheter is used, confirm dressing, flushing, infection precautions and eventual removal arrangements. Confirm spill or leak instructions and who to call if the pump alarms, becomes disconnected or appears to finish early. Never change settings or disconnect it yourself.</p></article><article class="access-step"><svg viewbox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#087b7a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="18" y="22" width="64" height="60" rx="7"></rect><path d="M18 39h64M33 15v16m34-16v16m-35 30 12 11 23-23"></path></svg><span>STEP 4</span><h3>Home support</h3><p>Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before each infusion is required for anti-sickness treatment and appears to help reduce liver toxicity. This regimen has moderate nausea/vomiting risk; agree the complete prevention and rescue prescription without duplicate steroids. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, a white-cell growth factor, may be used for low counts under local policy; it is not universally required.</p></article><article class="access-step"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="review" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 8h29v49H10V8h9M19 5h20v8H19Z"/><path d="m18 24 3 3 6-7m6 4h8m-23 13 3 3 6-7m6 4h8M18 48h23"/></svg></span><span>STEP 5</span><h3>Next appointment</h3><p>Confirm the next treatment, tests and any scan before leaving.</p></article></div><div class="access-details"></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="checks"><h2>Tests and reviews</h2><p class="pk-lead">Each test has a specific purpose. Use the individual test cards in the monitoring section.</p><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>Your next tests</h3><p>Before leaving, confirm which tests are due, where they will be done and who reviews the results before treatment.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Safety is different from benefit</h3><p>Normal safety blood tests do not show whether the tumour is responding. Your specialist sets the separate scan, symptom and function review.</p></article></div></section></section><section class="book44-chapter" id="book44-care"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="3"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 03</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Symptoms and supportive care</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">Recognising changes, knowing when to call, and using your prescribed supportive-care plan.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>NCCP 00374</p></header><section class="pk-section" id="cycle"><div id="wikiCycleChronology" class="wiki-cycle-map" data-cycle-days="21" style="--cycle-days:21"><h3>Day 1 → Day 21: a general pattern</h3><p class="wiki-cycle-intro">This is a visual orientation, not a prediction of your personal cycle. It shows when some effects often emerge and which effects are <strong>not</strong> tied to a particular day.</p><div class="wiki-cycle-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="General 21-day treatment pattern"><table class="book44-timeline"><thead><tr><th>Treatment or check</th><th>Timing in this view</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-span-start="0" data-span-stop="2"><th>Trabectedin: 24-hour infusion</th><td><svg class="book44-timing-lane" viewbox="0 0 480 42" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Days 1–2"><line x1="12" y1="21" x2="468" y2="21" stroke="#cbdedc" stroke-width="3"></line><rect x="12.0" y="14" width="43.4286" height="14" rx="4" fill="#8acfc8" stroke="#087e80"></rect><text x="12" y="10" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">1</text><text x="468" y="10" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">21</text></svg><strong>Days 1–2</strong><p>Starts on day 1 and continues into day 2, for 24 hours from the actual connection time, not two full days. The team confirms professional disconnection.</p></td></tr><tr data-span-start="0" data-span-stop="21"><th>Checks and symptom reporting</th><td><svg class="book44-timing-lane" viewbox="0 0 480 42" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Days 1–21"><line x1="12" y1="21" x2="468" y2="21" stroke="#cbdedc" stroke-width="3"></line><rect x="12.0" y="14" width="456.0" height="14" rx="4" fill="#8acfc8" stroke="#087e80"></rect><text x="12" y="10" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">1</text><text x="468" y="10" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">21</text></svg><strong>Days 1–21</strong><p>Keep the individual test schedule and report symptoms promptly. Safety checks and tumour-response reviews have different purposes.</p></td></tr><tr data-span-start="0" data-span-stop="1"><th>Dexamethasone premedication</th><td><svg class="book44-timing-lane" viewbox="0 0 480 42" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Day 1"><line x1="12" y1="21" x2="468" y2="21" stroke="#cbdedc" stroke-width="3"></line><rect x="12.0" y="14" width="21.7143" height="14" rx="4" fill="#8acfc8" stroke="#087e80"></rect><text x="12" y="10" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">1</text><text x="468" y="10" text-anchor="end" font-size="10" fill="#526b77">21</text></svg><strong>Day 1</strong><p>Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before trabectedin; reconcile the complete anti-sickness prescription.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="wiki-cycle-key"><div><strong>Schedule scope:</strong> This is later-line single-agent trabectedin over 24 hours. It is not first-line doxorubicin plus trabectedin with maintenance, and it is not the shorter ovarian-cancer combination infusion. Severe kidney impairment, heart problems or previous serious toxicity require individual oncology/pharmacy review before treatment can continue.</div><div><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Symptoms can occur on any day. An unshaded day is not a guarantee that treatment effects have passed.</div></div><div class="wiki-cycle-evidence"><strong>Why these bands stay broad:</strong> Illustrative treatment days, not dated appointments or guaranteed safe days. Your team confirms the actual doses, delays, support and appointments. Band length does not show likelihood or severity, and the chart cannot tell you whether it is safe to receive treatment. <a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Cancer Control Programme 00374, version 4a ↗</a></div><p class="wiki-cycle-warning"><strong>There are no guaranteed “safe days”.</strong> Follow your own prescription and hospital instructions. This information supports a discussion; it does not authorise treatment.</p></div><details open="open"><summary>Between-treatment notes</summary><h3>Day 1–2: Trabectedin: 24-hour infusion</h3><p>Starts on day 1 and continues into day 2, for 24 hours from the actual connection time, not two full days. The team confirms professional disconnection. Follow your confirmed appointment and support plan.</p><h3>Day 3–21: Between scheduled chemotherapy doses</h3><p>Continue the prescribed support and monitoring plan. Side effects can occur between visits; contact oncology promptly if unwell. Follow your confirmed appointment and support plan.</p></details></section><section class="pk-section" id="support"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Make the plan practical</div><h2>Support around the medicines</h2><div class="pk-two pk-support"><article class="pk-card"><h3 class="sc44-visual-label"><span class="sc44-icon-group" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="sc44-icon" data-sc44-icon="medicine" viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="#087780" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m17 15 6-6a12 12 0 0 1 17 17l-6 6ZM17 15 7 25a12 12 0 0 0 17 17l10-10M17 15l17 17"/><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="12"/><path d="m37 53 16-16"/></svg></span><span>Preparation and support medicines</span></h3><p>Review all medicines and supplements, especially strong liver-enzyme inhibitors or inducers, statins and other liver-toxic medicines. Avoid alcohol. Confirm steroid premedication, blood tests, pump training, line care, disconnection and verified contact numbers. Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before each infusion is required for anti-sickness treatment and appears to help reduce liver toxicity. This regimen has moderate nausea/vomiting risk; agree the complete prevention and rescue prescription without duplicate steroids. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, a white-cell growth factor, may be used for low counts under local policy; it is not universally required.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Fertility and pregnancy precautions</h3><p>Discuss fertility preservation before treatment. Avoid pregnancy and obtain written contraception and breastfeeding instructions for every medicine, including how long precautions continue after the last dose. Do not assume all drugs have the same waiting period. Contact oncology promptly if pregnancy is possible.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="support"><h2>Support and daily life</h2><p class="pk-lead">Follow the written plan agreed with your team.</p><div class="pk-two"></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="urgent"><h2>Symptoms and help</h2><p class="pk-lead">Report new or worsening symptoms promptly.</p><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>During a drip</h3><p>Tell the nurse immediately about flushing, rash, chills, breathlessness, faintness or pain/swelling at the infusion site.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Your contacts</h3><p>Keep verified daytime and out-of-hours oncology numbers with your treatment card. The quick response code website is information, not an emergency service.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-chapter" id="consent"><h2>Your treatment decision</h2><p class="pk-lead">Read the general chemotherapy risks and the individual medicine risks together.</p><div class="pk-two"><article class="pk-card"><h3>General chemotherapy risks</h3><p>Discuss low blood counts with serious infection or sepsis, anaemia and bleeding; nausea or vomiting; mouth soreness, appetite or taste changes, diarrhoea or constipation; fatigue, hair, nail and skin changes; changes in sexual wellbeing; allergic reactions and intravenous-line complications; blood clots, including clots in the legs or lungs; fertility, pregnancy and breastfeeding effects; and the possibility of lasting organ injury, rare later cancers or death from serious treatment complications. The likelihood and severity differ between medicines and people. Use this general discussion together with every medicine-specific risk below.</p></article><article class="pk-card"><h3>Your priorities and alternatives</h3><p>Discuss realistic benefit, uncertainty, alternatives including observation or symptom-focused care when appropriate, serious and lasting risks, personal priorities and the option to decline or reconsider treatment. This is later-line single-agent trabectedin over 24 hours. It is not first-line doxorubicin plus trabectedin with maintenance, and it is not the shorter ovarian-cancer combination infusion. Severe kidney impairment, heart problems or previous serious toxicity require individual oncology/pharmacy review before treatment can continue.</p></article></div></section><section class="pk-section pk-faq" id="questions"><div class="pk-eyebrow">Prepare for the conversation</div><h2>Questions worth asking</h2><details open="open"><summary>What is the treatment trying to do?</summary><p>The aim is to control advanced sarcoma and help symptoms. The strongest trial evidence concerns previously treated liposarcoma and leiomyosarcoma; the team explains expected benefit for your particular subtype. A specialist sarcoma multidisciplinary team reviews the exact pathology, disease extent, prior treatment and your priorities. Surgery or radiotherapy can have a separate role in selected situations. Symptom control, rehabilitation and palliative care can accompany systemic treatment; they are not restricted to the end of life.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Does a delay mean treatment has failed?</summary><p>No. Blood counts, organ function and symptoms can make a delay or dose change safer. Your team decides whether and when to give the next dose.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Who do I contact if unwell?</summary><p>Use your verified oncology daytime or out-of-hours number. If you cannot reach the team, seek urgent medical assessment. Severe symptoms require 112/999.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Can I restart after a delay or safety hold?</summary><p>Only after the treating team gives an explicit plan. A normal result does not erase a previous severe reaction or permanent-stop decision.</p></details><details open="open"><summary>Are safety tests the same as checking benefit?</summary><p>No. Safety tests look for treatment effects on the body. Scans, symptoms and function help the specialist assess tumour control.</p></details><div class="pk-note"><strong>Try explaining the plan back:</strong> Which medicines are given on each treatment day?,Which symptoms mean you should call immediately?,When are your next tests and review?</div></section><section class="book44-phase" data-support-source="support-day1.html"><div class="notice"><strong>Supportive prescription draft for review.</strong> This is a regimen template. It is not an issued patient prescription; individual review and completion are required.</div><h2>Day 1: trabectedin</h2><p><strong>Active medicines:</strong> Trabectedin</p><p><strong>Emetogenic risk:</strong> moderate</p><div class="support-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Supportive medicines: medicine, dose and timing"><table><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Dose / route</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dexamethasone</td><td>20 mg intravenously</td><td>30 minutes before every trabectedin infusion<small>Required anti-sickness premedication that appears to help reduce liver toxicity. Do not add the generic 8 mg steroid reference on top; reconcile all support steroids.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Individual anti-sickness prescription</td><td>Prescribed by the oncology team</td><td>The team specifies prevention, delayed-sickness and rescue timing<small>Reconcile interactions and the actual support prescription; this reference does not authorise self-treatment.</small></td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class="local-support-reference"><h3>Supplied local prescription reference</h3><p><strong>CUH Department of Medical Oncology: Adjuvant medication prescription for chemotherapy Moderate Risk for nausea and vomiting, Version 2 (05/05/2021)</strong></p><p>The local CUH source is retained as a reference list, not as the national antiemetic standard. Nystatin/chlorhexidine are displayed as local mouth-care adjuncts, not universal antiemetic or mucositis prophylaxis. Verify current local oral-care policy. This historical local proforma needs current pharmacy confirmation. It is an alternative reference, not a second course to add automatically.</p><div class="support-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Supportive medicines: medicine, dose and timing"><table><thead><tr><th>Medicine</th><th>Dose / route / frequency</th><th>Timing and directions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dexamethasone</td><td>8 mg by mouth once daily</td><td>Days 2-3<small>Take once daily for two days starting the day after chemotherapy.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Omeprazole</td><td>20 mg by mouth once daily</td><td>Days 1-3<small>Take one capsule daily for three days starting on the day of chemotherapy.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Nystatin</td><td>100,000 units/mL, 1 mL by mouth four times daily</td><td>Continuous during chemotherapy<small>Rinse 1 mL around the mouth four times daily after meals during the chemotherapy regimen.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Chlorhexidine mouthwash</td><td>10 mL by mouth four times daily</td><td>Continuous during chemotherapy<small>Rinse 10 mL around the mouth for at least 1 minute four times daily; expel after rinsing.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Cyclizine</td><td>50 mg by mouth three times daily as required</td><td>As required during chemotherapy<small>Take one tablet three times daily as required.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Metoclopramide</td><td>10 mg by mouth three times daily as required</td><td>As required during chemotherapy<small>Take one tablet three times daily as required.</small></td></tr><tr><td>Loperamide</td><td>4 mg once initially, then 2 mg by mouth four times daily as required</td><td>Chemotherapy-induced diarrhoea; max 16 mg/day<small>Take 4 mg after the first episode of diarrhoea, then 2 mg after each further episode, to a maximum of 16 mg/day.</small><small class="local-source-clarification">Source clarification: the supplied CUH reference gives both four-times-daily as-needed and after-each-episode dosing. Oncology/pharmacy must confirm one schedule before use. Follow the individual prescription; 16 mg/day is a maximum, not a target.</small></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><h3>Additional phase-specific support</h3><ul><li>Dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before each infusion is required for anti-sickness treatment and appears to help reduce liver toxicity. This regimen has moderate nausea/vomiting risk; agree the complete prevention and rescue prescription without duplicate steroids. Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor, a white-cell growth factor, may be used for low counts under local policy; it is not universally required.</li><li>Review all medicines and supplements, especially strong liver-enzyme inhibitors or inducers, statins and other liver-toxic medicines. Avoid alcohol. Confirm steroid premedication, blood tests, pump training, line care, disconnection and verified contact numbers.</li><li>The team chooses appropriate intravenous access and explains its care. Report pain, burning, redness or swelling during treatment immediately. If a port or peripherally inserted central catheter is used, confirm dressing, flushing, infection precautions and eventual removal arrangements. Confirm spill or leak instructions and who to call if the pump alarms, becomes disconnected or appears to finish early. Never change settings or disconnect it yourself.</li><li>An improved test result does not authorise restarting after a safety hold; confirm the new prescription.</li><li>Olanzapine may be added for breakthrough nausea/vomiting; cyclizine may be considered where the patient is not already receiving olanzapine.</li><li>Only optional additional antiemetic steroid may be adjusted after prescription reconciliation. Retain the required dexamethasone 20 mg intravenously 30 minutes before every trabectedin infusion; the generic omission advice does not apply to this required premedication.</li><li>Metoclopramide: adult maximum 10 mg up to three times daily (30 mg/day) and maximum treatment duration 5 days under current European Medicines Agency/Irish product information; avoid prolonged repeated use without review.</li></ul><p><strong>Before signing:</strong> verify allergies, current medicines, interactions, renal / hepatic function, QT risk, sedation, prior response, total steroid exposure and the rescue-medicine duration. Do not duplicate medicines from another phase.</p><p>Individual changes / rescue duration: __________________________________<br>Prescriber / date: __________________ Pharmacy review: __________________</p></section></section><section class="book44-reference-anatomy" id="anatomical-effects" data-book44-anatomy-source="index.html"><h2>Your anatomical side-effect map</h2><section class="patient-bodymap-card hcc-bodymap regimen-anatomy" id="side-effects" data-authored-map data-regimen-map="00374"><div class="hcc-visual-title"><h4>A picture of side effects</h4><p>Match each symptom box to its numbered body location. Tell your team about new symptoms.</p></div><div class="hcc-visual-stage"><svg class="hcc-static-connectors" aria-hidden="true" viewbox="0 0 100 100" preserveaspectratio="none"><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="12.5" x2="50" y2="11.8625"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="12.5" x2="49.28" y2="22.8125"></line><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="37.5" x2="47.48" y2="30.1125"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="37.5" x2="46.22" y2="35.2225"></line><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="62.5" x2="55.04" y2="50.1875"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="62.5" x2="50" y2="40.6063"></line><line stroke="#682b9f" stroke-width=".42" class="immune" x1="32" y1="87.5" x2="55.4" y2="63.8750"></line><line stroke="#a94022" stroke-width=".42" class="chemo" x1="68" y1="87.5" x2="44.6" y2="70.2625"></line></svg><div class="hcc-organ-column"><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="mouth"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">1</span><strong>Mouth and appetite</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report mouth ulcers, soreness or trouble drinking early. Use the prescribed mouth-care and anti-sickness plan.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="liver"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">3</span><strong>Liver</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Blood tests detect liver effects. Yellow eyes, dark urine or new significant upper abdominal pain needs prompt assessment.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="blood"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">5</span><strong>Blood cells</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Low counts can cause infection, anaemia or bleeding. Fever, shaking chills or sudden illness needs immediate oncology contact.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card left atezo" data-hcc-target="muscle"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">7</span><strong>Muscles</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>New marked muscle pain or weakness needs urgent review. Blood tests check for muscle injury even without symptoms.</p></article></div><div class="hcc-anatomy"><div class="hcc-image-wrap"><img src="../../assets/patient/anatomy-hcc-v0762.png" alt="Full-body anatomical illustration with numbered symptom locations"><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="mouth" style="left:50%;top:13%"><span>1</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="heart" style="left:48%;top:25%"><span>2</span></span><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="liver" style="left:43%;top:33%"><span>3</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="kidneys" style="left:39.5%;top:38.6%"><span>4</span></span><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="blood" style="left:64%;top:55%"><span>5</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="bowel" style="left:50%;top:44.5%"><span>6</span></span><span class="hcc-pin atezo" data-hcc-pin="muscle" style="left:65%;top:70%"><span>7</span></span><span class="hcc-pin bev" data-hcc-pin="whole" style="left:35%;top:77%"><span>8</span></span></div></div><div class="hcc-organ-column"><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="heart"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">2</span><strong>Heart and fluid leakage</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report new breathlessness, ankle swelling, faintness or rapid weight gain urgently. Heart tests continue during treatment.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="kidneys"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">4</span><strong>Kidneys and urine</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Muscle breakdown can damage kidneys. Dark or reduced urine, especially with muscle pain, needs urgent assessment.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="bowel"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">6</span><strong>Bowel</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report persistent diarrhoea or constipation, severe pain, abdominal swelling or inability to drink.</p></article><article class="hcc-organ-card right bev" data-hcc-target="whole"><div class="hcc-organ-head"><span class="hcc-card-number">8</span><strong>Infusion line and daily life</strong></div><div class="hcc-drug-label">Trabectedin</div><p>Report line-site pain, burning or swelling immediately. Keep the pump and line-care plan; do not alter settings yourself.</p></article></div></div><div class="hcc-visual-foot">Selected effects, not every possible effect or a prediction. Blood-count, skin and line markers are schematic. Number and text carry meaning; colour is decorative.</div></section></section><section class="book44-chapter book44-sources"><header class="book44-chapter-head book45-chapter-opener" data-book45-chapter="4"><p class="book45-brand">SACTCheck</p><div class="book45-opener-body"><p class="book44-chapter-kicker">CHAPTER 04</p><h1 class="book44-chapter-title">Sources and further information</h1><p class="book45-purpose-label">In this chapter</p><p class="book45-purpose">The references behind this information and addresses you can use to find further help.</p></div><p class="book45-regimen">Trabectedin for advanced soft tissue sarcoma<br>National Cancer Control Programme (NCCP) 00374</p></header><p>Use this information alongside the plan agreed with your treatment team. Your team confirms your medicines, doses and appointments.</p><ol class="book44-refs"><li><span class="book44-source-label">NCCP 00374 v4a · pTreatment, monitoring, administration and supportive-care sections</span><a href="https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf">https://healthservice.hse.ie/documents/6729/374_Trabectedin_monotherapy.pdf</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">eviQ: port</span><a href="https://www.eviq.org.au/p/507">https://www.eviq.org.au/p/507</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">eviQ: peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC)</span><a href="https://www.eviq.org.au/p/543">https://www.eviq.org.au/p/543</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">eviQ: pump</span><a href="https://www.eviq.org.au/p/502">https://www.eviq.org.au/p/502</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">Trabectedin versus dacarbazine in previously treated liposarcoma or leiomyosarcoma</span><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5070559/">https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5070559/</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">European Medicines Agency: Yondelis product information</span><a href="https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/product-information/yondelis-epar-product-information_en.pdf">https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/product-information/yondelis-epar-product-information_en.pdf</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">Final overall-survival analysis of trabectedin versus dacarbazine</span><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31173362/">https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31173362/</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">National Cancer Institute: soft tissue sarcoma treatment context</span><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/types/soft-tissue-sarcoma/hp/adult-soft-tissue-treatment-pdq">https://www.cancer.gov/types/soft-tissue-sarcoma/hp/adult-soft-tissue-treatment-pdq</a></li><li><span class="book44-source-label">National Cancer Institute: palliative and supportive care alongside cancer treatment</span><a href="https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet">https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/care-choices/palliative-care-fact-sheet</a></li></ol></section></main></body></html>